.TH SISTA-ANSI 3 "" "Sista" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
sista-ansi \- terminal styling, screen, and cursor facilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sista/ansi.hpp>
.B #include <sista/cursor.hpp>
.PP
.B c++ program.cpp \-lSista
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The ANSI facilities emit terminal control sequences for foreground and
background colors, text attributes, cursor movement, screen erasure, and
selected terminal modes.
.PP
.B ForegroundColor,
.B BackgroundColor,
and
.B Attribute
represent the standard named settings.
.B RGBColor
and the integer overloads provide true-color and 256-color forms.
.B ANSISettings
groups a foreground color, background color, and one or more attributes so
the same appearance can be applied repeatedly.
.PP
.B Cursor
provides relative and absolute movement operations.
Cursor objects also manage cursor visibility during their lifetime.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <iostream>
#include <sista/sista.hpp>

int main()
{
    sista::ANSISettings warning(
        sista::ForegroundColor::YELLOW,
        sista::BackgroundColor::BLACK,
        sista::Attribute::BRIGHT);

    warning.apply();
    std::cout << "warning";
    sista::resetAnsi();
    std::cout << '\en';
}
.fi
.SH PORTABILITY
Terminal capabilities vary.
Applications should not assume that every color, attribute, or screen mode
is supported by every terminal or redirected output stream.
Reset changed settings before returning control to the shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sista (3),
.BR sista-field (3)
